.TH STUD 1 "September 2026" "Stud @STUD_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
stud \- play Roblox on Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stud
.RI [ URI ]
.br
.B stud \-\-settings
.br
.B stud \-\-install\-desktop\-entry
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Stud
runs the real, unmodified Roblox Android application on a Linux desktop, in
its own window, with the mouse and keyboard. There is no browser, no
emulator and no virtual machine: Stud loads Roblox's own engine and supplies
the Android framework, window, input devices and graphics stack itself.
.PP
Roblox is not included. You supply the Android application package yourself,
and Roblox remains subject to its own terms. Point Stud at it in Settings
before the first launch.
.PP
Run with no arguments, Stud launches the application. Given a
.B roblox:
or
.B roblox\-player:
URI \- what a browser hands over when you press Play on the website \- it
launches straight into that experience.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-settings
Open the Settings window on its own, without launching the application.
This is what the desktop entry's Settings action runs.
.TP
.B \-\-install\-desktop\-entry
Register Stud as the handler for
.B roblox:
URIs for the current user, so a browser can hand a game over to it. A
package installs the desktop entry system-wide already; this exists for
running Stud from a build tree.
.PP
Stud also re-executes itself with several internal options
.RB ( \-\-game\-info ,
.BR \-\-notify ,
.BR \-\-notify\-region ,
.BR \-\-store\-secret ,
.BR \-\-load\-secret ).
They are implementation details, not a supported interface.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/stud/config.json
Settings. Written by the Settings window, and safe to edit by hand.
.TP
.I ~/.config/stud/flags.json
Roblox engine flag overrides, if any. Hand-edited only: Stud never
generates this file.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/stud/
Stud's own data, including the engine's files directory.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/stud/
The extracted application package and the engine's caches. Regenerable:
deleting it costs a re-extraction, nothing else.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/stud/logs/
One log per session, from all three of Stud's processes. The last ten are
kept.
.SH NOTES
The login is kept in the system keyring through the Secret Service
interface, never in a file of Stud's own.
.SH BUGS
Report at
.UR https://github.com/CatPieLeaf/Stud/issues
.UE .
